From 31c12e56c6b6c772f727af8ab31685e48a934d20 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Strata layer <layer@studioz.local>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 01:20:38 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] layer split: --batch-mtp across the stages, and
 --head-device picks the head's card

--batch-mtp ran on one GPU only, because a slot's draft state could not be reasoned about across a stage
hand-off.  With a layer split each stage already keeps its own session per slot, so the slot drafters now load on
the stage that runs the head - the last one - from that stage's slot session (its QSA geometry, its host copy and
its device), and bind that stage's weights and head with their residual buffers on its device.  The window's
residual is the last stage's (final_R_all chains through the pipeline), and the draft K/V copies - at admission,
and back to the solo path - run with the drafter's device current.  The PLE session stays the first card's slot
session, where the PLE table lives, as the main drafter's does.  With one GPU bslot_ss.size() == 1, so the stage
index is 0 and the lines are the ones they always were.

The two decisions behind it are pure functions in core/stage_plan.hpp, exercised host-only by
tests/core/stage_plan_test.cpp: whether --batch-mtp may run at all (a layer split no longer refuses it; every
other requirement, and --split-device 0, still do), and which device owns the head.

--head-device D (STRATA_HEAD_DEVICE) places the output head and, with it, the MTP draft layer - it reads the last
stage's residual, so the two move together - by reordering the later stages so D is last.  A device the pipeline
does not use, and the primary device of a split (it runs the first stage and the prompt path), are said in one
line and the placement stays as it was.  Moving the head to another CARD is the card ORDER: the server lists the
config's "head_device" (nvidia-smi numbering) last in CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES, so the engine's last stage is that
card - no engine flag is involved, so an older engine understands it too - and setup --head-device N|auto picks
that card by the estimated milliseconds per layer (SMs x clock, from the installed engine's own
`strata-device --list-devices`) instead of by free VRAM, printing the estimate for the chosen pair even when it
moves nothing.

Inert by default, asserted in the host test: --head-device -1 returns the device order untouched, and the batch
rule still refuses everything it refused before.
